 <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed text-sm mb-4 speakable">A wireless charger isn't automatically an FCC ID product. Part 18 consumer ISM equipment may use SDoC or certification, but a data radio changes the path. Check the KDB 680106 conditions, the US responsible party, and the exact-model test report before you approve a purchase order.</p>
 <ul class="text-sm text-slate-700 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
 <li><strong>Part 18 offers two paths:</strong> 47 CFR §18.203 allows SDoC or certification for consumer ISM equipment, so "wireless" alone doesn't decide the path.</li>
 <li><strong>KDB 680106 conditions are cumulative:</strong> below 1 MHz, up to 15 W per coil, direct contact, mobile exposure, and field strength below 50% of the MPE limit.</li>
 <li><strong>Data changes the answer:</strong> Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, NFC, or telemetry needs Part 15 certification; dual authorization is common for hybrid devices.</li>
 <li><strong>SDoC needs a US responsible party:</strong> under 47 CFR §2.909, the importer can be that party, and the statement must accompany the product.</li>
 <li><strong>Qi isn't FCC:</strong> Qi/WPC certification is a separate interoperability mark and doesn't replace an SDoC statement or FCC ID.</li>
 </ul>
