 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. The Short Answer: FCC ID vs SDoC for Wireless Chargers</h2>
 <p class="text-slate-700 font-semibold leading-relaxed mb-4">Direct answer: a wireless charger isn't automatically an FCC ID product. Under 47 CFR Part 18, a consumer wireless power transfer device may be authorized through either Supplier's Declaration of Conformity (SDoC) or certification.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The right path depends on the charger's operating frequency, power per coil, exposure conditions, and whether it also transmits data. If the charger includes Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, NFC, or data telemetry, the data radio needs Part 15 certification. Many supplier quotes get this wrong because they treat every wireless charger as an intentional radiator that must have an FCC ID.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>SDoC</strong> is a supplier's declaration backed by testing. The responsible party signs the compliance statement, keeps the test records, and supplies the statement with the product. There's no FCC ID number and no TCB grant. For SDoC products, the responsible party must be located in the United States.</p>
 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Certification (FCC ID)</strong> is a review by a Telecommunications Certification Body. The product receives an FCC ID, appears in the FCC equipment authorization database, and carries a grant that identifies the grantee, model, and rule parts. Certification is the usual path for intentional radiators that transmit information, such as Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, or NFC radios.</p>

 <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl">
 <table class="w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-xl text-sm overflow-hidden">
 <thead class="bg-brandBlue text-white">
 <tr><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Product Scenario</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Likely FCC Path</th><th class="text-left p-3 font-black uppercase text-xs">Why</th></tr>
 </thead>
 <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Wireless charging only; below 1 MHz; up to 15 W per coil; direct contact; mobile exposure; below 50% MPE; no data</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Part 18 SDoC or certification</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">§18.203 permits either path; KDB 680106 conditions are met</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Wireless charging outside the KDB conditions: higher power, higher frequency, at-a-distance, or portable exposure</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">KDB inquiry; certification likely</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The exclusion criteria are not met</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Wireless charging plus Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, NFC, or data telemetry</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Part 15 certification for the data radio; Part 18 for charging</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The data radio is a Part 15 intentional radiator; dual authorization is possible</td></tr>
 <tr class="border-t border-slate-200 bg-white"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm">Wired charger or adapter only, with no RF transmission</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">Part 15B SDoC</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm">The device is an unintentional radiator</td></tr>
 <tr><td class="p-3 font-bold text-sm bg-white">Qi/WPC certified, but no FCC documentation</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm bg-white">Not enough</td><td class="p-3 text-slate-600 text-sm bg-white">Qi/WPC is an interoperability mark, not an FCC authorization</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>

 <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Answer those four questions before you compare prices.</p>

