<!-- 9. FAQ -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<section id="faq" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
 <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
 <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm mb-6 text-center max-w-3xl mx-auto">The most common questions from US importers and brand owners about FCC ID vs SDoC for wireless chargers, answered with the rule text and the importer duties that apply:</p>

 <div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">

 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Does a Qi wireless charger need an FCC ID?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Not automatically. A Qi charger that transfers power only and meets the KDB 680106 conditions may use Part 18 SDoC or certification. If it includes Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, NFC, or data telemetry, the data radio needs Part 15 certification. The Qi/WPC mark is separate from the FCC authorization.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can a wireless charger use SDoC?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Yes, when it is a Part 18 consumer ISM device and the design meets the applicable conditions. SDoC is not available for the data radio in a hybrid device. The responsible party must be located in the United States, and the compliance statement must accompany the product.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What is the difference between FCC ID and SDoC?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">SDoC is a supplier's declaration backed by testing; there is no FCC ID and no TCB grant. Certification is a TCB review that issues an FCC ID and lists the product in the FCC database. SDoC is common for Part 18 chargers and Part 15 unintentional radiators. Certification is common for intentional radiators that transmit information.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Who is the responsible party for SDoC?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Under 47 CFR §2.909(b), the responsible party must be located in the United States. It can be the manufacturer, the assembler, or the importer. If you import the product, you may be the responsible party even if the factory prepared the test report.</p>
 </div>

 <div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
 <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Is Qi certification the same as FCC?</h3>
 <p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">No. Qi/WPC certification is a voluntary interoperability mark. FCC authorization is a legal requirement for RF devices in the US market. A Qi certificate doesn't replace an SDoC statement or an FCC ID.</p>
 </div>

 </div>
 </div>
 </div>
</section>

